Date: Tue, 18 Jan 1994 01:13:42 -0800
From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: Extropian love

Here are a few comments which may at first seem to be unrelated, but
which I hope to mold into somewhat of a coherent thread:

1.  Threads.  Note that I'm posting this as part of the original
"Extropian love" thread.  This topic has blossomed (mushroom-clouded?)
into several disparate areas such as: the nature of relationships,
families, psychotherapy, religion, and the nature of Extropianism.
While I think this is good, I wonder if these different topics might
all be different aspects of the same thing, and it might be good to
try to sew them back together.  Furthermore, it might make things
easier on those who subscribe to the digest, as all posts will be
accumulated under the same subject heading, rather than several
different ones.  Perhaps "Extropian love" isn't the best overall
topic, but it will serve for now.

2.  Relationships.  It seems to me that the people on this List do
form several relationships among themselves, whether they are the
"touchy-feely" kind or not.  If we confine ourselves to a mathematical
definition of "relationship" (i.e. "a proposition which is true for a
given set of free variables") then it becomes evident that there are
many such statements which are true for different subsets of people on
the List.  An exhaustive list of such propositions is probably not
useful, but an examination of the process by which such relationships
are formed, and the evolutionary pressures to which the memesets they
represent are subjected, is certainly in order.  I think we have all
recently witnessed this kind of evolution in action.

3.  Boundaries.  If we consider the set of propositions and
corresponding sets of free variables (i.e. people) for which those
propositions hold to be equivalence classes, then we can also consider
the concept of "boundaries" to be topologies which define who is part
of a given class.  Of course, an individual may be a member of several
different equivalence classes at once.  These boundary-topologies then
become decision processes for determining (a) whether a given
individual is part of a given relationship, (b) whether a given
behavior is appropriate for a given relationship, and (c) what the
basic assumptions (or expectations) of a given relationship might be.
Looked at in this way, items in (b) might be considered statements in a
formal system, while items in (c) might be considered the axioms of
that system.  The process of testing boundaries might then be
analagous to finding a proof for a member of (b) which can be
constructed from (c).  If no such proof exists, then we might say that
a behavior is "inappropriate" for a given relationship, or that a
particular relationship boundary has been crossed.

4.  Principles.  It is not necessary to agree with the principles
which codify a formal system in order to use them to construct proofs,
or, equivalently, to test the boundaries of relationships within that
system.  I do not necessarily agree with all of the ideas expressed in
v2.5, but yet I accept that they do form a basis for decision
processes which revolve around the concept of "Extropianism".
Furthermore, I have an expectation that these Principles can be used
by others to construct the same set of valid statements in the
Extropian system, and that their invariance will filter out subjective
differences between individuals.  Through this process, I expect that
the validity of any given proposition can be tested and classified in
a coherent and consistent manner.  This includes statements I might
make concerning the nature and basis of relationships between given
individuals. 

5.  Religion.  Because it is based on faith rather than proof, because
it rejects the filtering process of rational analysis, and because it
admits subjective variation of conclusions formed from identical
information content, I do not include any "system" of religion in the
above. Therefore I do not include any relationship which is based on
religion in the above, and consider discussion of such relationships
in the same context as the above to be an inappropriate use of
analytical resources.

