Speaking of revelations, I welcome this revelation of Tim's perceptions and
expectations, regarding both what he thought I was trying to do, and what
he thought I expected to come of it.  It goes a ways towards explaining his
reactions.
>
>In the context of this list, I have dislikes for many of the personality
>characteristics of many on this list. Are things made better if I
>"share" these views with you?

Perhaps (not necessarily), if in the process you reveal to the persons of
whom you are speaking that your views jibe with those of others, and thus
that perhaps the characteristics in question may warrant scrutiny (cf. my
comment on Schelling points in perception space in another post).
>
>The joke "Thank you for sharing that with us" is common not only
>amongst laypersons like ourselves, but also amongst therapists I know.

I don't consider it a joke.  It acknowledges that the listener heard the
speaker, and that there is no other useful input to be given.  It can also
mean "Maybe you were expecting to get a rise out of me, but it won't
work.", or "I respectfully disagree", or any of a number of other things,
depending on context.  I find "Thanks for sharing" quite useful at times.
Actually, I somewhat expected this or its equivalent (not the scene
described above) to be the response to my rant; sometimes I miss.

>I've said it several times before and I'll say it again: some list
>members apparently see this list as a new kind social group therapy
>session, to do a "show and tell" in their progress toward "Recovery"
>(from whatever haunts them...drugs, Objectivism, oppressive parents,
>Lifespring, est, unhappy childhoods, religion,  etc.).

I am interested in the journeys of those who are transforming themselves in
an Extropic direction.  "From where" may be needed for background
(sometimes I talk too much about this part), but I'm most interested in "to
where" and "how".  This applies to everything from discussions of smart
drugs, to rethinking of epistemology, to attempts to create more Extropian
relationships, to learning how to survive in the post-cryptography world.
Without feelings attached, the journeys just look like dry collections of
facts, not like things that budding transhumans actually _do_.

Like it or not, we are still animals.  We have skin and guts, glands and
hormones, and denying it don't make it go away.  We also have judgement,
which some-of-us-yours-truly-included forget to use, but let's not throw
out the baby with the bathwater.  When people log into this list, they
don't leave their gonads or their adrenals in a drawer.  I think the key is
intelligent use, not abuse or disavowal.

