From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: Tech:  Net Tech

I use a Mac, and love it for its operating system.  If I want a command 
line script, I write Hypercard scripts.  For networking, however, I use IP.  
The Internet Protocol works between *all* computers, including PCs, Macs, 
Suns, Vaxes, etc.  My mail interface on the mac is graphical and button 
driven.  I see a list of mail, categorized by my setup, and I just click on 
the item I want.  I push the [Reply] button at the bottom to automatically 
reply.  None of this effects the protocol used to send the mail out over 
the network.  The user interface is pretty much independent of the network.

As for the server-type descriptions of folders and files being mounted as 
if they were on the local desktop, I would suggest NFS.  This is an IP 
protocol that is available on virtually all platforms.  NFS is included in 
PC/TCP and there is a version for MacTCP.  It exists in Sun and other 
Unixes.  Thus, PCs, Macs, Vaxes, Suns, and other machines can all mount the 
same disk, put icons on their desktop, and use the same protocol.

Using Appletalk protocols will appeal to MS-DOS/Windows users as much as 
using Workgroup-for-Windows will appeal to Mac users.  We need to converge 
on a real common-ground protocol, and not one of the "open-systems" that 
all of the vendors are promoting.  It seems every vendor has invented an 
"open-system" around their product and expects all other vendors to migrate 
to their solution.  Yeah, right!
