          to, to read the words of, and occasionally even to
          attend parties with. Some I find less interesting.
          But none of them do  I view as part of an
          "extended family" except in the most banal sense."
 
Kennita comments:"I found this sad until I forced myself to
     consider that he likes it that way; now I just find it
     puzzling.
 
Kennita, surely you respect Tim's freedom to have whatever
degree of relationship he wishes with whomever he wishes? I
leave others to find happiness in their own way. What use is
puzzling over them? Such puzzling sounds overly invasive,
even busybodyish to me. I have a wide range of relationship
to others on this list, from intimate friends to mere
acquaintances. What works for me is not what works for
everyone, and I need not explain my preferences to anyone.
 
More generally, I find that attentive etiquette has been  of
greater value to me in social situations (like this List)
than rudeness rationalized by "honesty", "directness",
"truthful self-revelation", or even "massive retaliation".
Dealing with other people's overflowing emotional garbage is
no treat. An assumption of intimacy does *not* mean that you
need not concern yourself with your communicant's
sensibilities.
 
Kennita asks: Is it also reasonable to request that when
     people find their boundaries being violated or pressed,
     they communicate that clearly, honestly, and directly?
     (As a communication fanatic, I realize that I likely
     have a different perspective on this than the norm.)
 
Have a care. Fanaticism often justifies outrageous behavior.
Showing respect for the sensibilities of others - even when
you are complaining about their incomprehensible behavior -
makes it more likely that they will respect yours. If others
continue to violate you, in polite society, there is always
the "cut direct" - just stop communicating or socializing
with them.
 
Kennita writes: "It's possible that the nun/God relationship
     can be viewed as a subset of such relationships, since
     the God she (or he, but then it's a monk) has the
     relationship with exists for her only as her own mind
     conceives Him/Her/It."
 
I have a wonderful relationship with the Easter Bunny, from
which I derive much pleasure, but have not so far revealed
it for fear that people will be confirmed in their belief
that I am mad.
 
Translation: the nun/God relationship is not Extropian,
because it is founded on an arational absurdity, though it
may have a temporary extropic effect for the nun. Perhaps
she has not yet developed a strong sense of self, meaning,
or purpose, and her "relationship" with God relieves a deep
psychical tension, and allows her to be happy. But her
"relationship" could also foster information-supressing
irrationality, stalling further personal evolution. As
Extropians, we do not assume unknowable, supernatural forces
that control our destiny; nor do we close our minds to
natural but as-yet-discovered forces that may have this
effect. "No mysteries are sacrosanct, no limits are
unquestionable."
 
 
