Perhaps this is sufficient to explain how Redwoods function, but I wonder
if there is another factor.  I've heard it claimed that coastal fog is
essential to Redwoods' survival.  This is not strictly true, as Redwoods
are quite popular as landscaping trees in the Central Valley, where there
is likely no fog, or any precipitation at all for that matter, for half
the year.

But it could be true for the really big trees; in fact, their natural
range suggests that it is.  Also, redwood branches droop, so I wonder if
a siphoning action has anything to do with it.  Or perhaps the fog
absorption is simply necessary to restore the water columns after they
break from cavitation.

Like Mike, out of my field--just speculating.

