Date: Tue, 18 Jan 1994 11:26:22 -0800
From: amara@netcom.com (Amara Graps)
Subject: HST fixed (we hope)

Steve Witham asked about observing the whole sky with the Hubble
Telescope (how long would it take and how much data would be generated).

I can't even give a ballpark answer because the question
wasn't specific enough.

	- Which instrument on Hubble? (and at what wavelength?)
	- At what resolution? (corollary: How big of a field-of-view?)

I can offer some information that may help, but I can't answer
his question specifically.

* The EOS satellite will generate ~1 TerraByte of data per day.

* The Palomar 48" Schmidt telecope that has performed two large
  all-sky surveys took a few years to do it the last time (in the mid-80's
  I think), and it was just the sky that they could see (not the
  Southern Hemisphere). They used glass photographic plates (which I hope 
  are digitized now) with a wide-field aperture. A typical 48" Schmidt
  wide-field photo is a few minutes to ~ 10 minutes of arc on each side.

* The sky is 4pi steradians.

* The diffraction limit (blurring of point sources) is 
  1.22 * lambda / D  where lambda is the observation wavelength
  and D is the diameter of the telescope.

  ==> for Hubble (2.4 m) in the visible (5000 angstrom) = 
      1.22 * (5000 * 10^{-10}) / 2.4 = 2.5*10^{-7} 
      Which is (*by 3600) about 9*10^{-4} minutes of arc. 

* Hubble is in a low earth orbit and has a strong dependency on 
  tele-communication via the Tracking and Data Relay Satellite System (TDRSS)

* Hubble's maximum daily transmittal rate is 2 Gigabytes.

* HST efficiency. (Info from the Dec '90 StScI newsletter) One of the big 
  unknowns in HST operations is the overall efficiency that HST will achieve. 
  TScience efficiency' is defined as the ratio of time spent on doing 
  scientific observations to total elapsed time; it is affected by orbital 
  constraints (60-65% loss) comprising earth occultations (47%), the South 
  Atlantic Anomaly (10%) and bright limb constraints (5-10%). Add to this the 
  programme dependent observatory overhead times, i.e.Fixed Head Star Tracker 
  updates (5 min each), guide star acquisition (8-20 min each), target 
  acquisition (0-25 min each) and science instrument overheads (0-3 min) and 
  a variety of other potential operational overhead times.

I can't really go any further without knowing specfications of the
instrument used to perform the observing and the answers to the questions
listed in the beginning.

