Mike Price comments:

> Perhaps African animals evolved greater intelligence in the presence
> of hominids.

  There seems to be other similar behavior of non-human animals
toward humans.  Consider lions, for example.  George Adamson has commented
-- I think it was in his marvelously titled _My_Pride_and_Joy_, though perhaps
it was quoted by Joy Adamson in _Born_Free_ or one of her other books -- that
prides of lions generally will get up and move off when Masai warriors come
within sight.  He also commented that in his opinion, African lions were
generally more ill-disposed toward African natives than toward persons of
European ancestry.  This last is possibly a genetic and/or cultural reflection
of the long coevolutionary history of specific populations of the two
species.  It would be interesting to know whether there were similar
attitudes among European, Asian and North American lions toward native
human populations, but I doubt sufficient records survive to tell.  (The
North American population was one of those that perished at the end of
the Pleistocene; European lions survived till historic times, and I think
there are still a few Indian lions remaining of the Asian populations.)

  Another interesting and widely misunderstood behavior is the fierce
confrontational snarl of a large predator, generally taken to mean something
like "I am about to pounce and eat you up!"  It seems more nearly to be the
case that such displays have to do with fear or dominance.  The snarling
animal either sees you as a threat -- likely in the case of smaller predators
-- or as a competitor -- likely in the case of larger ones.  In either case,
it is probably not so much interested in you as dinner as wishing you would
go away, which you can probably manage to do safely if you are circumspect
about it.  Of course, if you withdraw in too much haste, the predator may
decide that it had overestimated your combative abilities, and perhaps you
are an entree after all.

  The rule seems to be, that predators do not snarl at their prey any more
than you or I snarl at our morning bowl of Cream of Wheat -- it's not
necessary, and it might even be counterproductive, if the intended meal
should take fright and flee.  (Brief pause for contemplation of the Hobbsian
image -- that's Calvin-and-Hobbsian image -- of a large terrified gob of
hot cereal hastily hopping toward the far side of the breakfast table.
"Die, vile glop!")

  On the other hand, if a large predator approaches you slowly, with a
benign or neutral expression on its face, you are probably being stalked, and
are in serious trouble...

